<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4916"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 27, 2005 - 12:46 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Thanks, George!  You took the words right out of my.....well, never mind.  In any case, the idea of a female SK spoofing as a male would make a great episode for yet another profiler-type TV show.  And, undoubtedly, we will see it soon. <BR> <BR>Now, if SK spoofing in general is considered to be within the bounds of this thread, maybe I wouldn&#39;t mind hearing some more on the topic.  I would be curious what degree of systematic identity deception has ever been uncovered in prior cases, since it may apply here &#40;to lesser degrees, of course, than the original post postulates&#41; &#60;wink&#62;.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
