<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5023"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Nancydrew<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Nancydrew<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 27, 2005 - 10:37 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Has anyone looked at pics of Professor PJ?  All due respect, she&#39;s not the most &#34;delicate flower&#34; of a woman I&#39;ve ever seen.  I realize she&#39;s no longer alive, which would mean someone else has to make the drops, etc, but we&#39;ve explored that possibility before.  Also, her class schedules reflect a Monday, Wednesday, Friday work week, with Tues and Thurs off.  I also read somewhere that while she loved poetry, she wasn&#39;t much of a writer herself.  The &#34;Oh Anna&#34; poem could certainly be from one woman to another, as someone else mentioned in a post. <BR> <BR>One would have to take the Bright crime out of the &#34;definite victims&#34; category for it to fit...Other eyewitness accounts of a man could just be mistakes, or a disguised woman.  People are also quoted as talking about how private she was.   <BR> <BR>I know this is nutty, and a bit ridiculous, but just thought I&#39;d back up Tfoth a bit.   <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
