<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6307"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tfoth<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tfoth<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, February 05, 2005 - 9:52 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->AUDREYAMW: The MPD aspect is the one I was having a hard time getting to because of the female perp angle, which has now been eliminated so we can move forward.  I know MPD has often been used as a legal angle for defense but I think this could be the real deal.  I know MPD alters have committed violence &#40;often on themselves&#41;, I do not personally know of cases of alters committing murder, and I certainly don’t know of any serial killers who were definitely associated with the disorder.  That’s the very discussion I would hope would ensue.  Thanks for your post. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6307-->
<!--Post: 6329--><!--Time: 1107701464-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6329"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Audreyamw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Audreyamw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, February 06, 2005 - 6:51 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->OK, I&#39;ve done some Web surfing to read up on Dissociative Identity Disorder &#40;the pathology formerly known as MPD&#41;.  &#40;Katharine Ramsland has a particularly good article about DID as a criminal defense at crimelibrary.com.&#41; <BR> <BR>DID has been linked to violence, mainly when someone with DID tries to self-harm, although there may be cases where someone with DID has been violent towards others -- I don&#39;t think the latter situation has ever been proven beyond a shadow of a doubt, though. <BR> <BR>DID has never been effectively used as a defense in a SK case, although malingerers have tried it &#40;Kenneth Bianchi, who was one of the Hillside Stranglers, and Arthur Shawcross are notable examples of malingerers who have claimed DID as a defense for SK.&#41; <BR> <BR>Based on what I&#39;ve read so far on SK, DID, and psychopaths, IMHO it isn&#39;t clinically possible for someone to be both a sadistic SK and to have DID simultaneously.  It&#39;s been established that DID develops in children who have experienced &#40;or possibly only witnessed&#41; horrifying and overwhelming violence.  The child develops DID because they are intelligent, creative, and psychologically equipped somehow so that they are deeply suggestible.  The dissociation occurs as a way of escaping traumatic emotional flooding, if I can call it that.  People with DID often &#40;or always?&#41; have PTSD as well as DID. <BR> <BR>BTK&#39;s trail of victims reveals a few things that we do know for sure -- that BTK is sadistic, enjoys torturing victims, spends a lot of time at most scenes, is compulsive about killing, and has done it over and over.  These obvious facts point towards a psychopath as the perpetrator.  A psychopath is emotionally dulled &#40;this has been shown to be connected to possible organic causes&#41;, and experiences no remorse.  BTK even taunts WPD, the public and survivors with his crimes.  BTK is someone who needs to inflict a lot of pain in someone else in order to experience excitement.  His stalking and killing are ways to get a thrill.  It requires a strong stomach to do the sorts of things that BTK does, because they&#39;re the sorts of things that only psychopaths do. <BR> <BR>Is it possible for someone with DID to have a psychopath &#34;alter&#34;?  Or for a psychopath to experience DID?  I think the two conditions are mutually exclusive.  People with DID are trying to deal with trauma that they&#39;ve experienced, and the overwhelming emotional pain that has resulted from the trauma.  A psyhopath doesn&#39;t experience emotional pain -- to the point of being able to become a sadistic SK just to get cheap thrills.  It makes no sense, IMHO, that both conditions would exist in the same person at the same time. <BR> <BR>That&#39;s my take on it, based on fairly superficial research.   <BR> <BR>If BTK wants to use DID as a defense, he&#39;d better get with the program.  This ain&#39;t the Sybil era anymore! <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6329-->
<!--Post: 6351--><!--Time: 1107718032-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6351"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tfoth<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tfoth<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, February 06, 2005 - 11:27 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->AUDREYAMW:  Your research is far from superficial in fact it was excellent.  What you have pointed out in words and facts, and what I had not considered or addressed is: <BR> <BR>The Theory assumes a MPD “alter” could also be a full-blown psychopath.   <BR> <BR>I must agree that maybe the simultaneous existence of these disorders would not be clinically possible.  I don’t know.  What are the odds of that happening?   I am no Dr., I just play one on the internet.  Great stuff.   <BR> <BR>Is there a Doctor in the house? <BR> <BR>This theory has been controversial to say the least, as you may have noticed.  There is a book “Little Girl Fly Away” ISBN 0671780859, 1994, a DID study, that takes place in Wichita, Kansas around 1980 – Police search for years for a male perp who assaulted a woman with a knife, gets national attention, complete with composite drawings, male voices, witnesses seeing things, and in the end it turns out to be a woman &#40;the victim&#41;.  Same City &#40;Wichita&#41;, same taunting of same LE &#40;WPD&#41;, same TV Station Involved &#40;KAKE&#41;, and within same basic timeline &#40;1980ish&#41;.   <BR>Literally same time, same place, same station.  There was even a movie version around 1998.  It’s an interesting read, good DID ref, and even mentions the BTK case.  She was only violent to herself and it is certainly a “softer” disassociative state than what we are looking at in this case, but I wanted to point it out to you in exchange for your research time.  I think you might appreciate it as a good read. <BR> <BR>And yet in this case the theory of the possibility of a female perp or a MPD connection has been deemed ludicrous, way out there, impossible, and off the wall, and simply trouble making.  Proves truth might be stranger than fiction.  Maybe the background basis of this theory should be “Are those who choose to ignore history destined to repeat it?”  <BR> <BR>Anyway, I think you have pointed out a very valid, and potentially major “flaw” with the theory in this BTK case.  That being: Has there ever been a case of an “alter” actually being a stand-alone psychopath?  And as far as a defense, I think you’re right, I don’t think in this case there is any defense at all, MPD or not, that will be palatable to any jury.  Thanks again. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6351-->
<!--Post: 6378--><!--Time: 1107736078-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6378"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Angrywolf<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Angrywolf<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, February 06, 2005 - 4:27 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Tfoth..you are a brilliant thinker...and I hope you keep on posting...I feel BTK must be a man..but I am no longer sure about the DNA evidence....it may have been planted if the copious amounts people say were at the scenes were there..a normal male could not produce that much...I agree with you though that BTK is misdirecting the search for him away from who he really is but there&#39;s no way to get on the right track.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6378-->
